## Limitations

- The dollar level of every price is the census median value in {{ v('policy.anchor_year') }} moved by the regulator index. Self reported values run high or low by metro (the census value over the transaction values in the loan extract has a median of {{ v('ch1.self_report.median_ratio') }} across {{ v('ch1.self_report.metros') }} metros, from {{ v('ch1.self_report.p10') }} to {{ v('ch1.self_report.p90') }}), and the anchor carries that bias into every year; the sensitivity table bounds it.
- The payment is principal and interest on {{ v('policy.loan_term_years') }} year money with {{ v('policy.down_payment_share') }} down at the quarterly average rate; taxes, insurance and maintenance enter the breakeven and the workbook, not the headline share, so every share is understated by the same amount in every year.
- The census has no one year release for {{ v('data.acs.gap_year') }}; every series that crosses it breaks, and the borrower pool compares that year's loans with the prior year's incomes.
- The supply estimate is a lower bound in size: builders answer the price growth they are regressed on, and the simulator puts the attenuation at {{ v('recovery.supply_controlled.mean_bias') }} with the demand control and {{ v('recovery.supply_uncontrolled.mean_bias') }} without it under its conditions. The migration series is one demand shock, not all of them.
- The migration regression's pre pandemic control removes most of the planted reverse causality on the simulator, where households move for one reason with one lag; the placebo flags the strong reverse causality condition in {{ v('recovery.migration.strong.flag_rate') }} of worlds, and remote work moved demand without moving many people.
- Intervals are cluster robust with {{ v('ch4.clusters') }} state clusters on the panel regressions, read on the t distribution with one fewer degree of freedom than clusters; with few clusters the recovery study, not the formula, is the evidence on coverage.
- The breakeven paths are drawn from each metro's own past, which includes a decade nobody expects to repeat; graded as late arriving truth on the simulator, the estimate missed the realised breakeven by {{ v('recovery.breakeven_horizon.mean_bias') }} years on average.
- The lock in measure counts originations, so a metro whose owners refinanced twice at the bottom counts each refinance; the notebook keeps the version that counted refinances twice as a dead end, and turnover also fell because inventory fell.
- The check log stores a metro code, an income rounded to the nearest thousand, a down payment share, a rate and a horizon; it cannot say who asked, and the planted identifier scan proves nothing else is stored.
